WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.978 --> 00:00:03.337
(logo whooshes)

00:00:03.337 --> 00:00:06.670
(soft delightful music)

00:00:13.020 --> 00:00:15.990
- I am Daniela Rus and I'm
here with my PhD student,

00:00:15.990 --> 00:00:19.170
Johnson Wang and my postdoc Byungchul Kim

00:00:19.170 --> 00:00:21.420
and together we are building

00:00:21.420 --> 00:00:25.440
towards the future of robot
design and manufacturing,

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:29.250
specifically we're building
a system called DiffuseBot,

00:00:29.250 --> 00:00:32.460
the system combines the
best of generative AI

00:00:32.460 --> 00:00:35.910
with the best of physics-based simulation

00:00:35.910 --> 00:00:39.690
to create a generative AI
approach for mechanisms

00:00:39.690 --> 00:00:42.180
that can operate in the physical world.

00:00:42.180 --> 00:00:45.540
- This is an example of
a robot that's generated

00:00:45.540 --> 00:00:49.100
by DiffuseBot and its goal
is to try to move that box,

00:00:49.100 --> 00:00:51.450
so it generate motion
like this rolling motion

00:00:51.450 --> 00:00:52.770
to try to move the box,

00:00:52.770 --> 00:00:54.900
it kind of combine the generative power

00:00:54.900 --> 00:00:56.490
with physical utility.

00:00:56.490 --> 00:00:59.250
I first tried to build
a physical simulation

00:00:59.250 --> 00:01:02.550
that's try to provide, kind
of like a learning signal,

00:01:02.550 --> 00:01:06.480
for the generative models to
do the task in the simulation

00:01:06.480 --> 00:01:08.730
and afterwards we can actually fabricate

00:01:08.730 --> 00:01:10.800
as we using 3D carbon printer.

00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:13.146
So think about now you
wanna customize robots

00:01:13.146 --> 00:01:15.090
for a certain physical task,

00:01:15.090 --> 00:01:18.090
how long will you spend you
to actually make the robot?

00:01:18.090 --> 00:01:20.160
- [Byungchul] Maybe it requires two weeks

00:01:20.160 --> 00:01:21.900
to one month, maybe?

00:01:21.900 --> 00:01:24.060
- [Johnson] It took
roughly, probably 30 seconds

00:01:24.060 --> 00:01:26.190
to specify the high level specification

00:01:26.190 --> 00:01:30.510
and then it took roughly like
6 to 12 hour of computer time

00:01:30.510 --> 00:01:32.280
to actually optimize the robot

00:01:32.280 --> 00:01:34.950
and then I hand the design to Byungchul.

00:01:34.950 --> 00:01:37.320
- It requires four hours of 3D printing

00:01:37.320 --> 00:01:40.560
and after that I need about
30 minutes to one hour

00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:42.510
to post-process the printer.

00:01:42.510 --> 00:01:45.570
- So the entire pipeline
only takes like one hour

00:01:45.570 --> 00:01:47.400
of human time and rest of the hours

00:01:47.400 --> 00:01:49.470
we kind of outsource that to computer

00:01:49.470 --> 00:01:52.350
and this is the coolest
thing of DiffuseBot.

00:01:52.350 --> 00:01:54.930
Like the previous robot
where it try to move the box

00:01:54.930 --> 00:01:56.610
from left to right, this robot,

00:01:56.610 --> 00:02:00.510
we specify the high level
objective as a picking up object,

00:02:00.510 --> 00:02:02.520
the gripper that's automatically generated

00:02:02.520 --> 00:02:05.670
by DiffuseBot, using
diffusion generative AI,

00:02:05.670 --> 00:02:06.960
it can learn to know

00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:09.870
to generate this kind of
parallel gripper-like shape

00:02:09.870 --> 00:02:11.760
and that can allow it to actually achieve

00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:14.520
the physical utility,
which is clamp the object

00:02:14.520 --> 00:02:15.510
and then pick it up.

00:02:15.510 --> 00:02:16.977
We use the exact same pipeline,

00:02:16.977 --> 00:02:19.380
the only difference is,
basically we specify

00:02:19.380 --> 00:02:20.970
different high level objective,

00:02:20.970 --> 00:02:22.020
so basically we still have

00:02:22.020 --> 00:02:24.870
this kind of physical
assimilation in the computer

00:02:24.870 --> 00:02:27.600
and then it'll automatically
optimize the robot

00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:29.010
to this specific shape.

00:02:29.010 --> 00:02:31.920
The diffusion process actually
look like this, for example,

00:02:31.920 --> 00:02:34.170
it start from kind of like a random noise

00:02:34.170 --> 00:02:37.680
and then it gradually converge
to a gripper-like shape

00:02:37.680 --> 00:02:41.460
as we see in here and also
in the physical robots.

00:02:41.460 --> 00:02:44.610
The most limitations
happens in same to real gap,

00:02:44.610 --> 00:02:46.770
because of the material we are using,

00:02:46.770 --> 00:02:51.030
the stiffness is pretty large,
so it requires a large force

00:02:51.030 --> 00:02:52.080
to actually bend the griper

00:02:52.080 --> 00:02:55.770
and then generate desired
motion to pick up the objects.

00:02:55.770 --> 00:02:57.480
- [Byungchul] We have to
think about the stiffness

00:02:57.480 --> 00:03:01.770
of the robot, so I have
to redesign the robot body

00:03:01.770 --> 00:03:03.810
to make it softer.

00:03:03.810 --> 00:03:05.010
- There's still some kind of gap

00:03:05.010 --> 00:03:08.370
between the actual material
we use in the physical world

00:03:08.370 --> 00:03:10.050
and the simulation we build.

00:03:10.050 --> 00:03:12.690
So this kind of gap is kind of troublesome

00:03:12.690 --> 00:03:15.480
and we are now working to
improve this kind of gap

00:03:15.480 --> 00:03:17.670
so that we can actually
generate the robots

00:03:17.670 --> 00:03:21.750
that can, at least 95%,
replicate the functionality

00:03:21.750 --> 00:03:23.760
that can be achieved in a simulator.

00:03:23.760 --> 00:03:25.710
Now we are seeing here is a robot

00:03:25.710 --> 00:03:29.460
that a DiffuseBot
designed to do locomotion,

00:03:29.460 --> 00:03:31.350
DiffuseBot automatically find

00:03:31.350 --> 00:03:33.600
this kind of four leg creatures

00:03:33.600 --> 00:03:35.730
that can try to walk forward,

00:03:35.730 --> 00:03:37.650
it's kind of try to bend its leg

00:03:37.650 --> 00:03:40.770
and then try to use the
frictions against the ground,

00:03:40.770 --> 00:03:42.570
try to push forward its body.

00:03:42.570 --> 00:03:44.040
When we're making a robot,

00:03:44.040 --> 00:03:46.740
there's some imperfectness
during the fabrication,

00:03:46.740 --> 00:03:49.260
such that there is some kind of asymmetry

00:03:49.260 --> 00:03:51.240
between the two different robot body,

00:03:51.240 --> 00:03:54.540
which makes it try to move
forward but also rotating

00:03:54.540 --> 00:03:55.740
at the same times.

00:03:55.740 --> 00:03:58.590
And we found that the
damping issue of the material

00:03:58.590 --> 00:04:02.250
makes the robot cannot
achieve dynamic motion,

00:04:02.250 --> 00:04:04.770
it's just moving forward
in the simulation.

00:04:04.770 --> 00:04:06.150
- [Byungchul] When we changed the motor

00:04:06.150 --> 00:04:07.920
to the high torque density motor

00:04:07.920 --> 00:04:11.040
and if we think about the pushing force,

00:04:11.040 --> 00:04:15.120
because this actuation is done
by tendon driven mechanism,

00:04:15.120 --> 00:04:17.880
which can only generate the pulley pulls,

00:04:17.880 --> 00:04:21.600
so maybe we can change the
locomotion after that one.

00:04:21.600 --> 00:04:23.970
- [Johnson] The major issue
that we're solving now

00:04:23.970 --> 00:04:26.910
in our ongoing project is try to make sure

00:04:26.910 --> 00:04:29.010
the same to real gap is small enough

00:04:29.010 --> 00:04:31.470
such that whatever we can
achieve in the simulation

00:04:31.470 --> 00:04:33.873
can be replicated in the real world.

